  "attributionNote": "The messenger poem of Kālidāsa: a yakṣa banished from his home for a year asks a monsoon cloud to carry his message to his wife in the Himalayas, and the poem's 111 verses are the description of the route the cloud must take. GRETIL's e-text has the 111 verses of the standard recension and was input by the Digital Sanskrit Buddhist Canon Input Project.",
  "description": "The Meghadūta is one of the most famous poems in any Indian language: the exiled yakṣa on Mount Rāma first describes the cloud, then the journey it must make from the Vindhyas north over the Narmada and the Ganges plain to the Himalaya and Alakā, warning it of the cities, the mountains and the rivers it will pass, and finally gives it the message of a lover to a wife who has not slept, closing with the shared grief that makes a message a consolation. Its combination of landscape description and longing became a genre of its own — the sandeśa poems of the following centuries all derive from it.",
  "historicalContext": "Composed under the Guptas and translated and imitated across Asia, the poem was commented on by Mallinātha and by the whole tradition of Sanskrit poetics as the exemplar of the description of nature and of unfulfilled love; its verses on the cloud as a bearer of news were recited and set to music in every Indian language.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete as GRETIL publishes it: the 111 verses of the standard recension, streamed from GRETIL at read time and indexed in reading order, since the poem has no chapter division of its own. This is the original Sanskrit, not a translation.",
